Scope Model Behavior PCS (Process-Contention) M:1, M:N User-level library schedules; competition within process only SCS (System-Contention) 1:1 Kernel schedules directly on CPUs; competition system-wide <pre><code>pthread_attr_t attr;
pthread_attr_init(&amp;attr);
pthread_attr_setscope(&amp;attr, PTHREAD_SCOPE_SYSTEM);  // Linux default
</code></pre> <p>Silberschatz: "The contention scope defines the set of threads against which a thread competes for CPU time."</p>

Mode Behavior Danger Asynchronous Thread killed immediately at any instruction May hold mutex → deadlock; may have malloc'd memory → leak Deferred (default) Thread checks at cancellation points (<code>pthread_testcancel()</code>, <code>pthread_join()</code>, <code>pthread_cond_wait()</code>, <code>read()</code>, <code>write()</code>) Safe — thread cleans up before exiting <pre><code>// Deferred cancellation example
void *worker(void *arg) {
    pthread_setcancelstate(PTHREAD_CANCEL_ENABLE, NULL);
    pthread_setcanceltype(PTHREAD_CANCEL_DEFERRED, NULL);

    while (true) {
        do_work_chunk();
        pthread_testcancel();  // Explicit cancellation point
    }
}
</code></pre>

